<title>UK: UK Conservatives detail new spending commitments.</title>
<headline>UK Conservatives detail new spending commitments.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-04-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>Britain's ruling Conservative party on Wednesday said a small number of new spending commitments included in its election manifesto would be fully financed by savings or additional income.</p>
<p>A party briefing paper estimated the annual cost of the spending pledges by the end of the next five-year parliament as follows, in millions of pounds.</p>
<p>1. Basic Pension Plus (top-up provision) -- 90</p>
<p>2. A Respite Care Programme -- 53</p>
<p>3. Partnership scheme for long-term care -- 35</p>
<p>4. Help one in five schools become specialist schools - 35</p>
<p>5. Extend electronically monitored curfew orders -- 20</p>
<p>6. Further extension of closed-circuit TV -- 15</p>
<p>7. Crime Act costs -- 17.</p>
<p>TOTAL -- 265 million pounds.</p>
<p>Savings and extra income would come from:</p>
<p>8. Review of delays in criminal justice system - 70.</p>
<p>9. Auction of licences for new generation of mobile phones -- 200</p>
<p>10. Administrative pricing of radio spectrum licences -- 65.</p>
<p>TOTAL -- 335 million pounds.</p>
<p>-- London newsroom 44 171 542 7767</p>
